# Electrochemistry: Batteries, Electrolysis, and Rust

> Push current through a compound, or let a reaction push current
> through a wire — electrochemistry runs both ways. It plates your
> jewellery, starts your car, and rusts your gate.

## 1. Electrolytes and Nonelectrolytes

[definition]
An **electrolyte** — sodium chloride solution, molten KCl, dilute
H₂SO₄, NaOH — conducts because it dissociates into mobile ions. A
**nonelectrolyte** — sugar or alcohol solution — holds no ions and
does not conduct.
[/definition]

Electrolytic conduction is ion motion, unlike metallic conduction
by electrons.

## 2. Electrolysis: Splitting with Current

The electrode dictionary — never mix these up:

| Electrode | Process | Memory hook |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Anode | oxidation (electron loss) | AN OX |
| Cathode | reduction (electron gain) | RED CAT |

[example]
**Electrolysis of acidified water:** hydrogen bubbles at the
cathode, oxygen at the anode — the volume of H₂ is exactly twice
that of O₂, matching 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂.
[/example]

**Molten NaCl** gives sodium metal at the cathode and chlorine gas
at the anode. Electrolysis of **brine** (the chlor-alkali process)
delivers three products at once:

| Product | Where it appears | Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cl₂ | anode | PVC, disinfectants, water treatment |
| H₂ | cathode | fuel, margarine |
| NaOH | left in solution | soap, paper, textiles |

Overall: 2NaCl + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + Cl₂ + H₂.

## 3. Galvanic Cells: Chemistry Makes Current

@diagram tree id="daniell-cell" layout="tree" aria="Parts of the Daniell cell"
  "Daniell cell" -> "Zn anode in ZnSO4"
  "Daniell cell" -> "Cu cathode in CuSO4"
  "Daniell cell" -> "Salt bridge"
@/diagram

The **Daniell cell** pairs a zinc rod in ZnSO₄ solution with a
copper rod in CuSO₄, joined by a **salt bridge** that keeps both
solutions electrically neutral. Zinc dissolves (Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻)
while copper plates out (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu), and electrons stream
through the wire — a cell EMF of about 1.1 V.

[note]
In galvanic cells the anode is still the oxidation electrode — but
it is the negative terminal, opposite to electrolytic cells.
[/note]

## 4. Batteries

| Cell | Type | Chemistry highlights | Where |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Dry cell (Leclanché) | primary (single use) | zinc anode, MnO₂ cathode in NH₄Cl paste | torches, remotes |
| Lead-acid | secondary (rechargeable) | Pb and PbO₂ plates in H₂SO₄ | car starter motors |
| Lithium-ion | secondary | Li⁺ shuttles between electrodes; light, energy-dense | phones, laptops, EVs |
| Fuel cell | continuous feed | H₂ + O₂ → H₂O plus electricity and heat | spacecraft, buses |

## 5. Corrosion: Electrochemistry in Reverse

Rust is electrochemical: iron at anodic patches dissolves while
oxygen is reduced at cathodic patches, with moisture as the
electrolyte.

| Prevention | How it works |
| --- | --- |
| Galvanization | zinc coat; even scratched, the zinc corrodes first |
| Sacrificial anodes | Mg or Zn blocks bolted to ship hulls corrode instead |
| Alloying | stainless steel (Cr, Ni) resists rust outright |
| Barriers | paint, grease, plastic dip — keep air and water away |

## 6. Electroplating

[definition]
**Electroplating** deposits a thin metal layer by electrolysis:
the object to be plated is the cathode, the plating metal is the
anode, and the electrolyte is a solution of that metal's salt.
[/definition]

| Object being plated | Electrode | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the item (spoon, ring) | cathode | metal ions reduce onto its surface |
| the plating metal bar | anode | metal dissolves into the solution as ions |

Uses: chrome on car bumpers, silver on cutlery, tin on food cans.

## 7. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Anode does what? | oxidation — always |
| Electrolysis of water — gas ratio? | H₂ : O₂ = 2 : 1 by volume |
| Chlor-alkali products? | NaOH, Cl₂, H₂ |
| Rechargeable car battery? | lead-acid |
| Which electrode gets plated? | the cathode |
| Sacrificial ship blocks? | magnesium |
